% ============================================================================
% sign-element-color — the colour the federal standard specifies for each
% PART of a traffic sign (adj-facts-stdlib, TRANSPORTATION).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? sign_element_color(stop_sign, background, $C)
%       % red
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5) with THREE columns, so each row lowers
% to `sign_element_color(sign, element, color)` carrying the source
% citation. The third column is why the table exists.
%
% *** WHICH PART OF THE SIGN IS PART OF THE FACT. ***
%
% "A YIELD sign is red" is FALSE as stated. Its BACKGROUND is white; red is
% its border and its legend. A two-column `sign_color(sign, colour)` would
% flatten three different claims into one and would get the most
% recognisable sign in the country wrong — and it would carry a federal
% citation while doing it.
%
%     sign           background   legend   border
%     ------------   ----------   ------   ------
%     stop_sign      red          white    white
%     yield_sign     white        red      red
%     warning_sign   yellow       black    black
%
% Every one of those nine facts comes from three sentences that state all
% three elements explicitly. Nothing is inferred from a colour convention,
% and the reverse queries are the interesting ones: `? sign_element_color
% ($S, background, red)` returns STOP alone, while `? sign_element_color
% (yield_sign, $E, red)` returns BOTH the legend and the border.
%
% This is the same shape `water-share.adj` needed for its denominators. A
% share without its base is not a fact, and a colour without its element is
% not one either. The lesson generalises: WHEN A SOURCE STATES A VALUE
% RELATIVE TO SOMETHING, THAT SOMETHING IS A COLUMN.
%
% SIBLING TO `sign-shape.adj`, WHICH DELIBERATELY LEFT THIS AXIS ALONE. That
% library's header records that colour "is a different axis and deserves its
% own table rather than being smuggled into a shape column", and this is the
% follow-through. The two tables quote THE SAME THREE SENTENCES for
% different facts, which is honest: a sentence that states both a shape and
% a colour grounds a row in each relation.
%
% NOT the same relation as `traffic-lights.adj`. That table holds
% `traffic_light_meaning(color, meaning)` — the colour of a SIGNAL and what
% a driver must do. This one holds the colour of a SIGN's parts. They share
% the atoms `red`, `yellow` and `green`, and disagree about nothing, because
% they answer different questions about them.
%
% Provenance: three sentences from the 2009 MUTCD — STOP from Section
% 2B.05 and YIELD from 2B.08, both in Chapter 2B, "Regulatory Signs,
% Barricades, and Gates"; the warning default from Section 2C.03 in Chapter
% 2C, "Warning Signs And Object Markers", which carries its own locator.
%
% `trust authoritative` — the FHWA is the first-party federal publisher and
% this is the standard itself. Both pages were CONTENT-verified by RAW TEXT
% EXTRACTION, and every quoted sentence was confirmed verbatim against the
% RAW HTML, not merely against the extraction: the pages wrap figure
% references in `<a>` tags, and a tag-stripping artifact previously put
% "(see Figure 2B-1 )" — with a space the manual does not contain — into a
% sibling library's citation before review caught it.
%
% Provenance is TABLE-level, so all nine rows carry all three sentences and
% `--explain` shows only the primary one. Issue #13898; the JSON output is
% the authoritative view.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - THE WARNING ROWS ARE FOR THE CLASS, NOT FOR ANY PARTICULAR WARNING
%     SIGN. The sentence describes what warning signs are, subject to its
%     own exceptions, so `warning_sign` is the atom and
%     `no_passing_zone_sign` gets no colour rows here — its sentence
%     specifies a shape and says nothing about its colours.
%   - `regulatory_sign` as a CLASS gets no colour rows. The rectangular
%     default sentence says nothing about colour, and STOP and YIELD prove
%     regulatory signs do not share one colour scheme anyway.
%   - The "WIDE" in YIELD's "wide red border" is a WIDTH, not a colour, and
%     is not recorded here. The border's colour is red; how wide it is
%     belongs to a dimensions relation this library does not hold.
%   - Retroreflectivity, fluorescent alternatives and the reserved colours
%     coral and light blue are discussed in Chapter 2A, largely inside
%     HTML TABLES that tag-stripping cannot quote byte-faithfully. A figure
%     this library cannot cite byte-faithfully is one it does not state.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table sign_element_color {
    columns sign, element, color

    row (stop_sign, background, red)
    row (stop_sign, legend, white)
    row (stop_sign, border, white)

    row (yield_sign, background, white)
    row (yield_sign, legend, red)
    row (yield_sign, border, red)

    row (warning_sign, background, yellow)
    row (warning_sign, legend, black)
    row (warning_sign, border, black)

    source "The STOP sign shall be an octagon with a white legend and border on a red background."
    locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part2/part2b.htm"
    trust authoritative
    cites "The YIELD (R1-2) sign (see Figure 2B-1) shall be a downward-pointing equilateral triangle with a wide red border and the legend YIELD in red on a white background." locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part2/part2b.htm"
    cites "Except as provided in Paragraph 2 or unless specifically designated otherwise, all warning signs shall be diamond-shaped (square with one diagonal vertical) with a black legend and border on a yellow background." locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part2/part2c.htm"
}
